From 01f471aeaa7c8e2a9c1f853634870c683251fa9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pascal Nasahl <nasahlpa@lowrisc.org>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 08:22:57 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] [rtl] Include RF write data into lockstep comparison

A fault into the main core could change a xor into a xnor (or similar).
In this case, the computed result is the inverse of the non-faulty
result. As the ECC of fault and non-faulty result are identical, this
fault can get unnoticed in the split register file inside the lockstep.
There, we only store the faulty data of the main core in the main core
RF and the ECC of the shadow core in the shadow core RF, which matches.

To mitigate this, compare the RF write data that is proced in the main
and shadow core in the lockstep comparison.

This adds 2x38-bits for the main core + 1x38-bits for the shadow core,
i.e., 114 FFs.

Signed-off-by: Pascal Nasahl <nasahlpa@lowrisc.org>
(cherry picked from commit 64a2b6581da7bf646ba80cfa4eca4f456a428b7f)
---
 03_reference/security.rst | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/03_reference/security.rst b/03_reference/security.rst
index 3ea0076536..df35e50e73 100644
--- a/03_reference/security.rst
+++ b/03_reference/security.rst
@@ -82,6 +82,8 @@ Register file ECC
 -----------------
 
 When Ibex is configured with the SecureIbex parameter, ECC checking is added to all reads of the register file.
+In addition, the register file write port (write address, write enable, and write data) of the main core is compared against the one of the shadow core in the dual core lockstep.
+Relying on the ECC check alone would miss a faulty result whose check bits happen to match those of the correct result, e.g., a bitwise inverted result.
 This can be useful to detect fault injection attacks since the register file covers a reasonably large area.
 No attempt is made to correct detected errors, but an internal major alert is signaled for the system to take action.
 
